Rotor and propeller performance in edgewise flight has attracted considerable attention recently due to the wide
spread use of fixed or variable-pitch propellers in modern air taxi and unmanned aerial vehicles1–3. Compared to
fixed-wing aircraft, which are more suitable for long flights in open environments4–6, multicopters have advantages for short duration missions in open or confined spaces due to their simplicity, maneuverability and vertical
take-off capability7. The steady and unsteady flow phenomena around the rotor blade can be investigated through
experimental8 or various numerical methods. The numerical methods can be further classified into 1-D Blade Element Momentum (BEM) methods and 3-D Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) techniques. Recently, CFD
analysis has been shown to predict the efficiency of hovering rotors9 accurately. The hover condition is a critical design point because of its high power consumption and the challenge of predicting the strength and position of
the vortex core10. The CFD technique can accurately handle a rotating blade’s complex unsteady flow phenomena. Although it is computationally expensive, the CFD technique has been widely used as an effective analysis tool
in academia due to the rapid advancement of High-Performance Computing (HPC) clusters11. According to the
literature related to CFD, Lanzafame et al.12 performed CFD analysis for performance prediction of the NREL
Phase IV rotor. Bai et al.13 developed a BEM model for small rotor blades, successfully validated through CFD
analysis. Tong et al.14 studied the effects of blade airfoil chord length and rotor diameter on the aerodynamic
performance of straight-bladed vertical-axis turbines. Yoon and Lee15 investigated the aerodynamic parameter analysis and design of a quadrotor air vehicle in hover. Their results indicated that optimized twist angle and
twist-starting position enable maximum hovering thrust in the proposed quad-copter. Lei and Wang16 studied the aerodynamic characteristics of a micro quadrotor aircraft considering the horizontal wind disturbance. They showed that the rotors’ thrust increases gradually with wind speed, resulting from increased pressure dif
ference between the upper and lower surfaces. Cai et al.17 simulated the unsteady aerodynamic configuration of a full-scale horizontal axis wind turbine with consideration of wind shear, tower shadow and yaw motion. Their results indicated that each blade simultaneously reaches its maximum and minimum aerodynamic loads during
the rotation cycle. Al-Ghriybah et al.18 studied the effect of the inner blade position on the rotor’s performance to determine the inner blade’s best position. The behavior of the blade tip vortices of a turbine equipped with a
brimmed diffuser shroud was investigated by Takahashi et al.19. Their simulation results indicated that a pair of vortices consisting of a blade tip vortex and a counter-rotating vortex was generated between the blade tip and the inner surface of the diffuser. The rotor was evaluated for steady and unsteady aerodynamics analysis under
surge motion20, yawed gusts21, ground effect22,23, and variable pitch24. Zhang et al.20 demonstrated that the mean power of the twin-rotor is increased by up to 13% under surge motion compared to the single rotor and that the small spacing ratio enhances the power gain. They revealed that surge motion significantly impacts the wake
interaction between the twin rotors and facilitates wake recovery. Cai and Gunasekaran21 examined the propeller performance under steady and sinusoidally time-varying freestream (stream-wise or longitudinal gust). They reported that the propeller thrust, power, pitching, and rolling moment increase with the incidence angle at the
same advance ratio. Tanabe et al.23 simulated a variable-pitch-controlled quadrotor drone using a high-fidelity CFD solver in the ground effect. They detected a flow recirculation for the quadrotor drone at an intermediate height of approximately 0.5 times the rotor diameter. Also, they observed that the required power of the quadrotor drone varies significantly with the rotor height from the ground and that a local power increase occurs around a rotor height of 0.5D. Nowadays, the use of artificial intelligence is being considered in all branches of
engineering25–27. In the literature, studies have been conducted to improve the efficiency of rotors by optimizing
blade shape28,29 or using flow control methods30–32. The effect of the functional parameters of the blowing and
suction jets on pitching airfoils in dynamic stall conditions was discussed by the authors in32–34. The authors30 recently optimized the blowing jet performance for a dynamic stall airfoil. They found that the optimal jet location was near the leading edge vortex formation, about 2 to 5 percent of the chord. The jet angle of 55 to 70
degrees improved the aerodynamic performance better. Salah et al.35 studied the performance of dual-rotor wind turbines (DRWT). They show that the DRWT significantly affects the efficiency of wind energy. Considerable research has been carried out on fixed and variable-pitch rotor blades, focusing on commercial blades featuring symmetrical blades like Naca0012 or Naca0015. Several studies have also been conducted on the NREL rotor. However, the authors did not find a study that works on the Combo KV29 rotor. On the other hand, most of the works investigated the steady state of the flow, and limited studies investigated the unsteady flow behavior. In the unsteady flow studies, limited cases were investigated due to the high computational time;
for example, Ali and Kim11 only investigated 3 cases of wind condition (without tilt, with tilt -20, and with yaw -20). On the other hand, numerical modeling of rotor aerodynamics and performance under wind poses several challenges. Therefore, the resulting rotor requires more in-depth studies for performance evaluation under different operational conditions. This study examined this rotor numerically under different winds for the first time. In addition, we tried to consider all the parameters and conduct a comprehensive study on wind’s effect on the rotor’s performance. In total, 16 different scenarios were considered for wind speed and direction, and a comprehensive study was conducted on the effect of wind on the performance of this rotor. Three-dimensional computational fluid dynamics was employed for this investigation. The motion is modeled as a dynamic mesh and the flow is solved unsteadily to investigate the physics of the flow more accurately. In the following sections, the governing equations of the problem are presented first. Then, the modeling approach is described, and after validating the solution and ensuring its accuracy, the performance of the rotor is examined in the presence of wind. Several wind scenarios are considered, and the effect of wind direction, angle, and speed on thrust and rotor torque is investigated. Flow physics is studied by examining contours and isosurfaces.


