Simulation set-up
U15XXL Combo KV29 industrial rotor blade was used. This is an industrial blade produced by T Motor Company. This engine is used in unmanned vehicles such as quadrotors. The reason for using it in this study was that the main and final goal is designing a quadrotor that is capable of transporting hospital equipment and supplies so that transportation in the hospital can be done more easily and quickly. For this reason, this industrial rotor was used, and aerodynamic analyses were performed on it in different conditions to design its control system. This rotor has features such as considerable power, a matched propulsion solution, stability, and reliability that are necessary for our quadrotor. The experimental model of this rotor was prepared, and then a computer-aided design (CAD) model of the rotor was created using Solidwork software. The CAD model was generated from a mesh file obtained by 3D laser scanning of the rotor. A view of this model can be seen in Fig. 1. This blade’s length, width, and depth are 62, 6.51, and 2.16 inches, respectively. In this study, a second-order two-dimensional finite volume method was applied, and a SIMPLE algorithm was utilized to couple pressure–velocity. Air density was considered as an ideal gas. The turbulent property was
modeled using URNAS models. The k-ω SST was used because of its reported accuracy in previous studies11. The discretization method adopted the second order upwind scheme. The internal iteration of 20 iterations each time
was selected. The simulation convergence was checked by monitoring residuals less than 10−6. We considered
