Computational domain & boundary conditions
Considering that the dynamic mesh model is supposed to be used, we must define the rotating computational zone near the model and the stationary zone in the rest. Therefore, the calculation area should be divided into two parts according to Fig. 2. In order to be able to use this computational area in the dynamic mesh method, the rotating zone is designed as a cylinder. The rotating zone is twice the length of the blade. The length of the stationary zone from the back of the blade is about 20 times the length of the blade, and from the front, it is 6 times the length of the blade. In Section "Domain independency", the domain independence is examined to ensure the sufficiency of the domain size. The boundary condition of the stationary zone’s side walls was considered a pressure outlet, the cross section near the blade was assumed as inlet velocity, and the end section of the cylinder was considered a pressure outlet. The wall with the no-slip condition was also considered on the blade. The boundary surface of the inner cylinder was also considered as the interface of two zones. In Fig. 2, computational domain and boundary conditions are specified. The mesh was created using the commercial software Pointwise. Structured and unstructured hybrid grids were used for meshing. On the surface of the blade, the main part of the blade was a meshed structure, but the tip of the blade and its hub were meshed in a triangular shape. The boundary layer was meshed as 10 cubic structured layers, and according to the selected turbulence model, the size of the first layer was considered such that y+ of this layer is about one. Except for the first ten layers related to the boundary layer, the mesh inside the computational zone was also considered unstructured. The T-rex feature of pointwise software has been used to build this mesh. In Fig. 3, a sample of the mesh inside the moving and fixed cylinder and the structured mesh
