Results and discussion Wind inflow effects on the performance of the turbine blade
The angle and direction of the wind’s interaction with the objects cause changes in the systems’ efficiency41–43. In different vehicles, the wind can hit the blade horizontally or vertically. For example, vertical wind with an angle is often observed in wind turbines or propeller motors. The horizontal wind is seen in devices such as quadrotors. On the other hand, in air vehicles with tilt-rotor change mechanisms, we see changes in the wind direction from horizontal to vertical and vice versa. Therefore, all these situations are considered, and the wind direction and angle effect are investigated in this study. In Section "The effect of wind direction ", the effect of wind direction on blade performance was investigated. Then, in Section "Effect of speed in lateral wind", the effect of speed in lateral wind was investigated and finally, the wind angle was examined on the blade’s performance. A total of 16 different cases were simulated, and the effect of wind on different conditions was investigated.
The effect of wind direction
In this section, we examined how wind direction affects blade performance. Wind turbines and quadrotors are examples of practical applications where side wind influences their blade efficiency and performance. The blade performance can improve or deteriorate depending on wind direction, angle and amplitude. Generally, wind can act on the blade in two ways: laterally (perpendicular to blade rotation direction) and longitudinally (along blade rotation direction), which can be either in the same direction or opposite to rotation. In the first scenario, we set wind speed to 5 m/s and considered three cases: lateral wind (CasePAR) , longitudinal wind in the same direction as rotation (CaseDAR) and longitudinal wind in the opposite direction of rotation (CaseODAR). Figure 9 shows a schematic of these three cases.
The manufacturer’s website of the KV29 industrial blade39 gives thrust and torque for throttles of 30, 60, and 90 percent. Based on the engine’s power, the rotational speed of these throttles can be measured, which was
available on the manufacturer’s website39 and is 896, 1592, and 2111 rpm, respectively. The percentage error has risen as the rotational speed has increased. This is because as the rotational speed increases, the interaction between the rotating area and the still air becomes more pronounced, leading to a more complex flow physics. With the increased complexity in the flow physics and heightened eddy interactions, the turbulence model struggles to predict flow behavior accurately. Since simulation gave the highest accuracy at 30% of throttle, we considered this rotational speed for investigating the blade under different wind conditions. Figure 10 illustrates the flow physics of these three cases. We plotted pressure contour along with velocity vectors in x and z planes at zero level for these three cases. As seen in z plane at zero level, when wind acts laterally (CasePAR) , there is more rotational flow, since flow does not enter perpendicularly to the plane. Wind may bend in that direction if it cannot resist flow induced by rotation, which completely happened in this case. According to figures in the x plane at zero level, when wind flow enters the same direction as rotation, its interaction with secondary flow induced by the rotating blade is low. As a result, the rotational region is less visible, but when wind flow enters
