Conclusions
This paper presents a CFD numerical study of the unsteady aerodynamic performance of a rotor blade under different wind conditions. The rotor blade used is the U15XXL Combo KV29 industrial blade, which has no available CAD model. Therefore, a 3D laser scanning technique was applied to generate the CAD model from a mesh file based on an experimental sample. A 3D transient CFD simulation using the dynamic mesh method was performed to analyze the rotor dynamics. The URANS equations with the k-ω SST turbulence model were solved to simulate the rotor with angular velocities of 863, 1592 and 2111 rpm under various wind directions, speeds and angles. The main findings of this study are summarized as follows.
(1) The study reveals that when the wind speed is insufficient to counter the secondary flow induced by the blade rotation, the thrust decreases across all wind directions. This finding has significant practical implications, particularly in scenarios where high-amplitude winds are infrequent. In such cases, the wind can actually reduce the thrust production by 3%, a factor that should be considered in calculations and operational planning.
