(2) The study demonstrates that lateral wind, a more common occurrence in quadrotor applications, has a less detrimental effect on thrust production compared to opposite wind. This comparative advantage of lateral wind provides reassurance and confidence in the performance of quadrotors in such conditions. (3) Increasing the lateral wind speed results in two peaks in the torque curve, forming a butterfly wing shape in the polar torque plot and multiple extrema in the torque curve with increasing speed. However, the thrust curve has only one peak. A closer inspection reveals that the thrust increases for all angles with increasing speed above 5 m/s, but this is not the case for torque. The maximum thrust shifts slightly to the left with increasing lateral wind speed. (4) The φ angle only causes a phase difference in the motion and does not affect the thrust amplitude and torque. Increasing the angle results in a regular process of changes, leading to more phase advances in the curves. (5) The ψ angle dampens the oscillatory motion in the blade motion. The motion amplitude decreases and the curve becomes smoother as the angle increases. A nearly straight line similar to no side wind is observed at 60 and 90 degrees, which is attributed to the constant effective angle of attack during the rotation cycle. The component opposite to the rotation direction increases as ψ angle increases, which causes a greater reduction in thrust compared to direct side wind.
